      ":question/id": "194833de-eabd-521e-a061-701c99c91a20",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-341858",
      ":question/problem": "Find the slope of $y=tan⁡x+2sin⁡x$ at $x=0.$\n\n${y}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sin⁡x)=cos⁡x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x+2sin⁡x) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡x+2cos⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(0)+2cos⁡(0) \\\\  & =1+2(1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $3.$"
    },
