      ":question/problem": "Find ${y}^{′}$ for $y=3({x}^{2}-2tan⁡x)+4.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3({x}^{2}-2tan⁡x)+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}-6tan⁡x+4) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2x-6{sec}^{2}⁡x+0 \\\\  & =6x-6{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
